# Scaladoc for methods

When writing or completing Scaladoc on methods, follow this format.

Document **every** method (`def`), wherever it is declared: classes, traits, case classes, standalone objects, companion objects, and test suites (abstract or concrete). There is no enclosing type that skips method Scaladoc. Class-level comments on companions are a separate concern (see `scaladoc-companions`).

## Required structure

1. **Summary** — 1–5 lines describing what the method does (not how). No empty `TODO` stubs.
2. **`@param name description`** — one tag per parameter; name must match the signature; include a short description after the name.
3. **`@tparam T description`** — one tag per type parameter when present.
4. **`@return description`** — required when the return type is not `Unit`; omit for `Unit` / side-effect-only methods.
5. **`@throws ExceptionType description`** — only when this method’s body **explicitly** throws (e.g. `throw new …` or a helper that always rethrows a documented exception from this method). Do **not** document exceptions that may bubble up from third-party libraries. Omit the tag entirely when there is no explicit `throw` in the method.
6. **`@since X.Y.Z`** — required on every **newly created** method. Set `X.Y.Z` to the current `ThisBuild / version` value in the project-root `build.sbt` (e.g. `"0.1.0"` → `@since 0.1.0`). Do not invent a version; read it from `build.sbt`.

Tag order: summary → `@param` → `@tparam` → `@return` → `@throws` → `@since`.

## Do / don't

```scala
// ❌ BAD — empty tags, missing descriptions, stale TODO, no @since;
//          @throws for a library call that may fail (not an explicit throw here)
/**
 * TODO
 * @param name
 * @param value
 * @throws IllegalArgumentException
 * @since
 */
protected final def createPart(name: String, value: String): Part[BasicBodyPart] =
  multipart(name, value)

// ✅ GOOD — no @throws (no explicit throw in the method body)
/**
 * Builds a single multipart form part from a name/value pair.
 *
 * @param name  form field name
 * @param value form field value
 * @return a multipart body part ready to attach to a request
 * @since 0.1.0
 */
protected final def createPart(name: String, value: String): Part[BasicBodyPart] =
  multipart(name, value)
```

```scala
// ✅ GOOD — @throws only because the method itself throws
/**
 * Parses a non-empty token from the input.
 *
 * @param raw raw token string
 * @return the trimmed token
 * @throws IllegalArgumentException if `raw` is blank
 * @since 0.1.0
 */
def requireToken(raw: String): String = {
  if (raw.trim.isEmpty) throw new IllegalArgumentException("token must be non-empty")
  raw.trim
}
```

```scala
// ❌ BAD — @return on Unit; missing @param descriptions
/**
 * Clears the buffer.
 * @return
 */
def clear(): Unit = ...

// ✅ GOOD — no @return for Unit
/**
 * Clears the buffer.
 *
 * @since 0.1.0
 */
def clear(): Unit = ...
```

## Test suites

Helpers, fixtures and factory methods in a test suite — including those on the suite companion — are documented exactly like production methods, even though a concrete suite (and its companion) carries no class-level Scaladoc (see `scaladoc-classes` and `scaladoc-companions`).

The `describe` / `it` bodies of a suite are test declarations, not methods, and are never given Scaladoc.

```scala
// ✅ GOOD — helper inside a concrete suite is still documented
class TokenSpec extends BasicSpec {

  /**
   * Creates a token with a fixed expiration for the tests in this suite.
   *
   * @param value raw token string
   * @return a token expiring one hour from now
   * @since 0.1.0
   */
  private def tokenOf(value: String): Token = Token(value, Instant.now().plusSeconds(3600))

  describe(anInstanceOf[Token]) {
    it("should expose its raw value") {
      tokenOf("abc").value shouldBe "abc"
    }
  }
}
```

## Notes

- Keep the summary factual and under 5 lines; prefer one short paragraph over long essays.
- Always fill in text after `@param`, `@return`, and `@throws` — bare tags are invalid.
- Never add `@throws` for exceptions raised by third-party APIs invoked inside the method.
- `@since` applies to **new** methods only; do not rewrite `@since` on existing methods unless the user asks.
- When completing incomplete Scaladoc (e.g. `TODO` / empty tags), apply this full template.
- Method Scaladoc is always required; the concrete/abstract and companion/standalone distinctions only affect **class-level** Scaladoc.
- Methods in companion objects and standalone objects use this same template (see `scaladoc-companions` and `scaladoc-classes`).
